# %% NORA task decomposition version 1.8 - one sequential pipeline, decomposition only
#
#   PHASE 1  Read the data          only the columns the decomposition needs (intent is never loaded)
#   PHASE 2  Prepare the calls      time order, each call counted once, system name, agent of each call
#   PHASE 3  Event log              every interaction as ordered steps (agent · API)
#   PHASE 4  Hand-off detection     calls with which the coordinator passes work to another agent
#   PHASE 5  The model              (a) which hand-offs bring work  (b) classes  (c) task DAGs
#   PHASE 6  Validation             k-fold cross-validation: learn on k-1 folds, test on the unseen fold
#   PHASE 7  Final model            refitted on all interactions
#   PHASE 8  Approved traces        follow their plan and are not anomalous (isolation forest)
#   PHASE 9  Outputs                decomposition workbook, validation workbook, report.md
#
# version 1.8: (1) the conversation is read as logged ({"type": "user", "data": {"content": ...}}): Input message = every
#      user message, complete; NORA replies = every assistant message, complete (1.7.x took the raw JSON text instead).
#   (2) Logged fields first: every field the log holds about the interaction itself - every column and JSON field of
#      the rows that are not tool calls (intent, intent_scope, user_inputs.*, feedbacks.* ...) (the user's request row, feedback rows) - is
#      found without a list typed in: a field with few different values is a column of Interactions, long text goes
#      to Interaction_Notes, identifiers are listed only in the validation workbook (Logged_Fields).
#   (3) Fields logged on the row of the user's message (what the user selected) are tested as triggers of extra calls
#      next to the input words (same Fisher + BH rule); fields logged later (feedback) are shown, never used as a cause.
#   (4) Classes / Class_Variants show the logged values of their interactions; Logged_Fields_vs_Class (validation) tests
#      how strongly each logged field goes with the class (Cramer's V, chi-square + BH, normalised mutual information).
# version 1.7.6: empty cells of the main workbook show '—' (Excel let the long Steps text spill into an empty neighbour cell).
# version 1.7.5: a step most interactions of a class run is part of that class's plan (same >50% rule as the standard plan);
#   input words that are English stop words (published scikit-learn list) are never triggers; an API name used by more than one
#   agent is shown with its agent; repeated-call values missing from a call show '(missing)'; Class_Variants shows the variant
#   typical calls; cells no longer wrap (Excel Online hid the second line).
# version 1.7.4: every cell of the main sheets holds only what the data says - API names, counts, the earlier result value or
#      input word found before an extra call, the input values that change between repeats, the earlier list a loop follows.
#      No sentences of ours in the cells; the meaning of each column is in the Guide sheet (last sheet).
# version 1.7.3: triggers must be STRONG (with the call at least twice as often as without it, and at least 30 points more),
#      values that are constant in one API's results are ignored (they only show that the API ran), a branch splits a class
#      only when at least 10% of the class takes it; every repeated call gets its driver from the logs (one call per item of
#      a list returned earlier, or per different value of an input field, or retries). Main sheets are plain words only;
#      every percentage, p-value and quality measure lives in the validation workbook.
# version 1.7.2: clean sheets - Interactions holds the complete input message, the steps in order (repeats shown ×n), branches,
#      counts, every repeated call and why it differs from its variant; Classes explain how each class and its variants are
#      identified; Class_Variants keeps only what defines a variant. Internal measures stay in the validation workbook.
# version 1.7.1: stricter triggers - present in MORE than half of the interactions with the new call and in LESS than half without
#      it (same rule as the hand-off test), echoed request parameters ignored, and the API whose result holds the trigger named;
#      loops shown per interaction and per class; variant typical = median of the variant's tool calls; misfit patterns per class.
# version 1.7: classes are split into VARIANTS by the conditional branches each interaction took (calls outside the plan with a
#      trigger proven in the logs); every interaction's call count is explained API by API against its variant's typical profile;
#      conditional steps are added to Task_Plan; the Interactions sheet comes first and holds the input. Approved traces and the
#      proofs (triggers, API count matrix, unexplained calls, misfits) are in the validation workbook.
# v7.8 answered "why is a NEW call added for the same class?": for every call that is not in the class plan, the trigger
#      found in the logs - an earlier call's result, or the input - tested with Fisher's exact test + BH (New_Calls_Why sheet).
# v7.7 explained, case by case, why interactions of the same class differ (named APIs as proof), and checks the classes honestly:
#      can the input predict the class / the experts? (Class_Check, Input_to_Class, Input_to_Handoff)
# v7.6 added the link input -> class: per class, its most frequent inputs and the words that stand out in them.
# v7.5 added, on request: the input of every interaction (first message + first tool-call input) and
# "why cases of the same class differ" - counted per interaction and summarised per class (Class_Variation sheet).
#
# Every agent, API, system, class and dependency comes from the data. Nothing is typed in by hand.
# The only fixed numbers are statistical conventions: "more than half", a 5% significance level, the Heuristics Miner default 0.9.

